.SS The 16-character comm limit
.PP
Without \fB\-f\fR, \fBpgrep\fR matches against \fIcomm\fR, which the
Linux kernel truncates to \fBTASK_COMM_LEN\fR (16) characters
including the terminating NUL — see \fBproc_pid_comm\fR(5). A process
launched as \fB./my\-very\-long\-program\fR therefore has \fIcomm\fR
\fBmy\-very\-long\-pr\fR, and \fBpgrep my\-very\-long\-program\fR
will not match it. Use \fB\-f\fR to match against the full command
line in such cases.
.SS Regex flavour
.PP
The pattern is a Rust \fBregex\fR-crate expression, which is broadly
compatible with POSIX extended regular expressions but is not
identical. Notable differences:
.IP \(bu 2
Backreferences (\fB\\1\fR, \fB\\2\fR, ...) are not supported.
.IP \(bu 2
The pattern is treated as an unanchored substring match by default;
use \fB^\fR / \fB$\fR to anchor it explicitly, or pass \fB\-x\fR for
a whole-string match.
.IP \(bu 2
Character classes such as \fB\\d\fR, \fB\\w\fR, \fB\\s\fR are
recognised in addition to POSIX classes like \fB[[:alpha:]]\fR.
.PP
\fB\-i\fR enables case-insensitive matching for the pattern only;
filter values (user names, terminals, etc.) are always matched
literally.
.SS Exit status
.PP
\fBpgrep\fR exits with:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB0\fR — at least one process matched.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fR — no processes matched.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2\fR — invalid invocation (typically: no \fIpattern\fR and no
filter flags supplied).
.PP
Output, when produced, is sorted ascending by PID. This is stable
across multiple invocations of \fBpgrep\fR against the same set of
processes.
